readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|Vnto mortall deth me to haue ye shold,
Ryght as a woman born here naturall,
A feminine thyng, woman at al houres,
To end of my days here ''terrestriall.''
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3622.}}}}

{{smaller block|There are also celestial bodies, and bodies ''terrestrial.''
{{right|1 Cor. xv. 40.}}}}

'''2.''' Representing or consisting of the earth: as, a or the ''terrestrial'' globe. See ''globe'', 4.

{{smaller block|What though, in solemn silence, all
Move round this dark, ''terrestrial'' ball?
{{right|''Addison'', Ode, The Spacious Firmament.}}}}

'''3.''' Pertaining to the world or to the present state; sublunary; worldly; mundane.

{{smaller block|A genius bright and base,
Of tow'ring talents and ''terrestrial'' aims.
{{right|''Young'', Night Thoughts, vi.}}}}

'''4.''' Pertaining to or consisting of land, as opposed to water, or of earth.

{{smaller block|The ''terrestrial'' substance, destitute of all liquor, remaineth alone. {{right|''Holland'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 598.}}}}

{{smaller block|I did not confine these observations to land, or ''terrestrial'' parts of the globe, but extended them to the fluids.
{{right|''Woodward.''}}}}

'''5.''' In ''zoöl.'', living on the ground; confined to the ground; not aquatic, arboreal, or aërial; terricolous. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In ''ornith.'', rasorial or cursorial; belonging to the ''Terrestres.'' (''b'') In ''conch.'', air-breathing or pulmonate, as a snail or a slug. (''c'') Belonging to that division of isopods which contains the wood-lice, sow-bugs, or land-slaters.}}

'''6.''' In ''bot.'', growing on land, not aquatic; growing in the ground, not on trees.—'''Terrestrial gravitation, magnetism, radiation, refraction, telescope.''' {{smaller|See the nouns.}}—'''Terrestrial-radiation thermometer.''' {{smaller|See ''thermometer.''}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' An inhabitant of the earth.

readings.c1s3:
[Obsolete or rare in both uses.]

'''terret, territ''' (ter′et, -it), ''n.'' [Origin obscure.] One of the round loops or rings on a harness-pad through which the driving-reins pass. See cuts under ''harness'' and ''pad-tree.''

'''terre-tenant, ter-tenant''' (tãr′-, tėr′ten″ant), ''n.'' [< OF. *''terre-tenant'', < ''terre'', land, + ''tenant'', holding: see ''terra'' and ''tenant.''] In ''law'', one who is seized of or has the actual possession of land as the owner thereof; the occupant.

'''terre verte''' (tãr vãrt). [F.: ''terre'', earth; ''verte'', fem. of ''vert'', green: see ''terra'' and ''vert.''] Same as ''terra verde'' (which see, under ''terra'').—{{smaller|'''Burnt terre verte''', an artists' color, obtained by heating the natural terre verte, changing it to a transparent muddy brown, with little or none of the original green tone remaining.}}

'''terrible''' (ter′i-bl), ''a.'' [< F. ''terrible'' = Pr. Sp. ''terrible'' = Pg. ''terrivel'' = It. ''terribile'', < L. ''terribilis'', frightful, < ''terrere'', frighten. Cf. ''terror'', ''deter.''] 1. That excites or is fitted to excite terror, fear, awe, or dread; awful; dreadful; formidable.

{{smaller block|''Terrible'' as an army with banners.
{{right|Cant. vi. 10.}}
Altogether it [a hurricane] looks very ''terrible'' and amazing, even beyond expression.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, II. iii. 71.}}}}

2. Excessive; tremendous; severe; great: chiefly used colloquially: as, a ''terrible'' bore.

{{smaller block|I began to be in a ''terrible'' fear of him, and to look upon myself as a dead man.
{{right|''Abp. Tillotson.''}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|The bracing air of the headland gives a ''terrible'' appetite.
{{right|''B. Taylor'', Lands of the Saracen, p. 20.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Terrible infant''', a noisy, rough, passionate, or inconveniently outspoken child [for F. ''enfant terrible''].}}

{{smaller block|Poor Reginald was not analytical, . . . like certain pedanticules who figure in story as children. He was a ''terrible infant'', not a horrible one.
{{right|''C. Reade'', Love me Little, i.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' Terrific, fearful, frightful, horrible, shocking, dire.}}

'''terribleness''' (ter′i-bl-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being terrible; dreadfulness; formidableness: as, the ''terribleness'' of a sight.

{{smaller block|Having quite lost the way of nobleness, he strove to climb to the height of ''terribleness.''
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, ii.}}}}

'''terriblize†''' (ter′i-blīz), ''v. i.'' [< ''terrible'' + ''-ize.''] To become terrible. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Both Camps approach, their bloudy rage doth rise,<br>And even the face of Cowards ''terriblize.''
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas's Weeks, ii., The Vocation.}}}}

'''terribly''' (ter′i-bli), ''adv.'' In a terrible manner. {{smaller|(''a'') In a manner to cause terror, dread, fright, or awe; dreadfully.}}

{{smaller block|When he ariseth to shake ''terribly'' the earth. Isa. ii. 21.}}

{{smaller|(''b'') Violently; exceedingly; greatly; very. [Chiefly colloq.]}}

{{smaller block|The poor man squalled ''terribly.''
{{right|''Swift'', Gulliver's Travels, i. 2.}}}}

'''Terricolæ''' (te-rik′ọ̄-lē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., pl. of L. ''terricola'', a dweller upon earth: see ''terricole.''] 1. In ''entom.'', a division of dipterous insects. ''Latreille'', 1809.—2. A group of annelids, containing the common earthworm and related

readings.c2s3:
terra, earth, land: see ''terra''. Cf. ''terrier''<sup>2</sup>.] One of several breeds of dogs, typically small, active, and hardy, named from their propensity to dig or scratch the ground in pursuit of their prey, and noted for their courage and the acuteness of their senses. {{smaller|Terriers are of many strains, and occur in two leading forms, one of which is shaggy, as the Skye, and the other close-haired, as the black-and-tan. They are much used to destroy rats, and some are specially trained to rat-killing as a sport.}}

{{smaller block|The eager Dogs are cheer'd with claps and cryes, . . .<br>And all the Earth rings with the ''Terryes'' yearning.
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas's Weeks, ii., The Decay.}}}}

{{smaller block|My ''terriers'',<br>As it appears, have seized on these old foxes.
{{right|''Massinger'', City Madam, v. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|The persecuted animals [rats] bolted above-ground; the ''terrier'' accounted for one, the keeper for another.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Vanity Fair, xlv.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Black-and-tan terrier''', the ordinary English terrier.—'''English terrier''', a general name of the smooth-haired terriers, of several breeds, as the common black-and-tan.—'''Fox-terrier''', one of different kinds of terriers trained or used to unearth foxes.—'''Maltese terrier''', a very small terrier, kept as a pet or toy.—'''Scotch terrier''', a general name of the shaggy lop-eared terriers, of several breeds, as the Skye, etc.—'''Skye terrier''', a variety of the Scotch terrier, of rather small size, and very shaggy.—'''Toy terrier.''' See ''toy''.—'''Yorkshire terrier''', a variety of the Scotch terrier. (See also ''bull-terrier, rat-terrier''.)}}

¶
'''terrier'''<sup>2</sup> (ter′i-ėr), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''terrar''; < OF. ''terrier'', in ''papier terrier'', a list of the names of a lord's tenants, < ML. ''terrarius'', as in ''terrarius liber'', a book in which landed property is

readings.c3s1:
described, < ''terrarius'', of land: see ''terrier''<sup>1</sup>.] In ''law'': (''a'') Formerly, a collection of acknowledgments of the vassals or tenants of a lordship, including the rents and services they owed to the lord, etc. (''b'') In modern usage, a book or roll in which the lands of private persons or corporations are described by their site, boundaries, number of acres, etc.

{{smaller block|In the Exchequer there is a ''terrar'' of all the glebe-lands in England, made about 11 Edward III. ''Cowell.'' (''Latham.'')}}

{{smaller block|It [Domesday] is a ''terrier'' of a gigantic manor, setting out the lands held in demesne by the lord and the lands held by his tenants under him.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Norman Conquest, V. 4.}}}}

'''terrier'''<sup>3</sup>† (ter′i-ėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''tarryour'', ''tarrere'', ''tarrer'', < OF. ''terriere'', ''tarriere'', ''tariere'', an auger, < *''tarrer'' (in pp. ''tarré'', ''taré''), bore, < L. ''terebrare'', bore: see ''terebrate''.] A borer, auger, or wimble. ''Cotgrave.''

{{smaller block|With ''tarrere'' or gymlet perce ye vpward the pipe ashore.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 121.}}}}

'''terrific''' (te-rif′ik), ''a.'' [= Sp. ''terrífico'' = Pg. It. ''terrifico'', < L. ''terrificus'', causing terror, < ''terrere'', frighten, terrify, + ''-ficus'', < ''facere'', make.] Causing terror; fitted to excite great fear or dread; dreadful: as, a ''terrific'' storm.

{{smaller block|The serpent . . . with brazen eyes<br>And hairy mane ''terrific.'' ''Milton'', P. L., vii. 497.}}

'''terrifical''' (te-rif′i-kạl), ''a.'' [< ''terrific'' + ''-al''.] Terrific. [Rare.]

'''terrifically''' (te-rif′i-kal-i), ''adv.'' In a terrific manner; terribly; frightfully.

'''terrifiedly''' (ter′i-fīd-li), ''adv.'' In a terrified manner.

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''terrify''' (ter′i-fī), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''terrified'', ppr. ''terrifying''. [= F. ''terrifier'' = Sp. Pg. ''terrificar'', < L. ''terrificare'', make afraid, terrify, < ''terrere'', frighten, + ''facere'', make (see ''-fy'').] '''1.''' To make afraid; strike with fear; affect or fill with terror; frighten; alarm.

{{smaller block|When ye shall hear of wars and commotions, be not ''terrified''.
{{right|Luke xxi. 9.}}}}

{{smaller block|This is the head of him whose name only
In former times did pilgrims ''terrify''.
{{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim's Progress, ii., Doubting Castle.}}}}

{{smaller block|Girls, sent their water-jars to fill,
Would come back pale, too ''terrified'' to cry,
Because they had but seen him from the hill.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 344.}}}}

'''2'''†. To make terrible.

{{smaller block|If the law, instead of aggravating and ''terrifying'' sin, shall give out license, it foils itself.
{{right|''Milton''.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' To scare, horrify, appal, daunt. See ''afraid''.}}

'''terrigenous''' (te-rij′e-nus), ''a.'' [< L. ''terrigena'', one born of the earth, < ''terra'', earth, + ''-genus'', produced: see ''-genous''.] Earth-born; produced by the earth.

{{smaller block|''Terrigenous'' deposits in deep water near land.
{{right|''Nature'', XXX. 84.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Terrigenous metals''', the metallic bases of the earth, as barium, aluminium, etc.}}

'''terrine''' (te-rēn′), ''n.'' [Also ''terrene'', ''terreen'', and corruptly ''tureen''; = G. ''terrine'', < F. ''terrine'', an earthen pan or jar, < ML. ''terrineus'', made of earth, < L. ''terra'', earth: see ''terra''.] '''1.''' An earthenware vessel, usually a covered jar, used for containing some fine comestible, and sold with its contents: as, a ''terrine'' of pâté de foie

readings.c3s3:
gras.

{{smaller block|Tables loaded with ''terrenes'', filigree, figures, and everything upon earth.
{{right|''H. Walpole.''}}}}

Specifically—2. An earthen vessel for soup; a tureen (which see).

{{smaller block|Instead of soup in a china ''terrene'', it would be a proper reproof to serve them up offal in a wooden trough.
{{right|''V. Knox'', Winter Evenings, lvii.}}}}

'''territ''', ''n.'' See ''terret.''

'''Territelæ''' (ter-i-tē′lē), ''n.'' Same as ''Territelaria.''

'''Territelaria''' (ter″i-tē-lā′ri-ä), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < L. ''terra'', ground, + ''tela'', web, + ''-aria''<sup>2</sup>.] A division of spiders, including those which spin underground webs for their nests, as a trap-door spider. {{smaller|The group contains all the tetrapneumonous forms, and corresponds to the ''Mygalidæ'', or theraphoses. Also ''Territelæ.''}}

'''territelarian''' (ter″i-tē-lā′ri-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to the ''Territelaria.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' Any member of this group.

'''territorial''' (ter-i-tō′ri-al), ''a.'' [= F. ''territorial'' = Sp. Pg. ''territorial'' = It. ''territoriale'', < LL. ''territorialis'', of or belonging to territory, < L. ''territorium'', territory: see ''territory.''] '''1.''' Of or pertaining to territory or land.

{{smaller block|The ''territorial'' acquisitions of the East-India Company . . . might be rendered another source of revenue.
{{right|''Adam Smith'', Wealth of Nations, v. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|A state's ''territorial'' right gives no power to the ruler to alienate a part of the territory in the way of barter or sale, as was done in feudal times.
{{right|''Woolsey'', Introd. to Inter. Law, § 52.}}}}
